1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:03,755
December 7, 1941.

2
00:00:04,025 --> 00:00:06,394
A date that would change the course of
history.

3
00:00:06,833 --> 00:00:12,058
Shortly before eight o'clock on a quiet
Sunday morning, the first Japanese
aircraft

4
00:00:12,108 --> 00:00:13,465
appeared over Oahu.

5
00:00:13,735 --> 00:00:19,045
Within two hours, much of the United
States Pacific Fleet lay burning in Pearl
Harbor.

6
00:00:19,315 --> 00:00:22,398
More than two thousand four hundred
Americans were dead.

7
00:00:23,067 --> 00:00:28,149
It was one of the most daring naval
operations ever attempted, and one of the
most

8
00:00:28,199 --> 00:00:30,915
consequential mistakes in modern history.

9
00:00:31,185 --> 00:00:37,010
To understand how it happened, we have to
go back ten years, and thousands of miles
to the west.

10
00:00:42,967 --> 00:00:47,028
In 1931, Japan's army seized Manchuria.

11
00:00:47,298 --> 00:00:52,547
Six years later, fighting near Beijing
escalated into full-scale war with China.

12
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:58,672
Japan's leaders sought an empire that
could supply what the home islands lacked:

13
00:00:58,722 --> 00:01:00,985
iron, rubber, and above all, oil.

14
00:01:01,255 --> 00:01:05,814
Japan imported most of its oil, the bulk
of it from the United States.

15
00:01:06,267 --> 00:01:12,643
In 1940, Japanese forces moved into
northern French Indochina, and Japan
signed the

16
00:01:12,693 --> 00:01:15,863
Tripartite Pact with Germany and Italy.

17
00:01:16,133 --> 00:01:22,669
Washington answered with economic
pressure, restricting exports of scrap
iron and aviation fuel.

18
00:01:23,633 --> 00:01:28,882
In July 1941, Japanese troops entered
southern Indochina.

19
00:01:29,152 --> 00:01:34,684
The United States froze Japanese assets,
and American oil exports to Japan stopped.

20
00:01:34,734 --> 00:01:38,313
Britain and the Dutch government in exile
followed.

21
00:01:39,267 --> 00:01:45,061
Tokyo faced a choice: give up its
conquests in China, or seize the oil
fields of the

22
00:01:45,111 --> 00:01:47,140
Dutch East Indies by force.

23
00:01:47,410 --> 00:01:53,301
And on the flank of any move south lay the
United States Pacific Fleet, based since

24
00:01:53,351 --> 00:01:55,004
1940 at Pearl Harbor.

25
00:01:58,567 --> 00:02:03,885
Admiral Isoroku Yamamoto, commander of
Japan's Combined Fleet, had studied at

26
00:02:03,935 --> 00:02:07,571
Harvard and served as naval attaché in
Washington.

27
00:02:07,841 --> 00:02:13,047
He knew America's industrial strength, and
he opposed war with the United States.

28
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:18,414
But if war came, he believed Japan's only
chance was to strike first: cripple the

29
00:02:18,464 --> 00:02:24,007
Pacific Fleet at the outset, and buy time
to seize the resources of Southeast Asia.

30
00:02:24,867 --> 00:02:31,775
In November 1940, British carrier aircraft
had crippled the Italian fleet at anchor
in Taranto.

31
00:02:32,045 --> 00:02:36,054
The lesson was clear: a fleet in harbor
was vulnerable from the air.

32
00:02:36,300 --> 00:02:38,816
Yamamoto was not the plan's only author.

33
00:02:39,086 --> 00:02:44,564
Air staff officer Minoru Genda worked out
much of the operational detail, Yamamoto's

34
00:02:44,614 --> 00:02:49,895
own staff refined it, and the Naval
General Staff approved it only after
Yamamoto

35
00:02:49,945 --> 00:02:51,477
threatened to resign.

36
00:02:52,333 --> 00:02:58,755
Pearl Harbor was shallow, only about forty
feet deep, too shallow for ordinary aerial
torpedoes.

37
00:02:59,025 --> 00:03:04,151
Japanese engineers fitted wooden fins to
their torpedoes, and aircrews trained for

38
00:03:04,201 --> 00:03:05,992
months over Kagoshima Bay.

39
00:03:09,533 --> 00:03:15,531
American cryptanalysts had broken Japan's
top diplomatic cipher, known to them as
Purple.

40
00:03:15,801 --> 00:03:21,387
The decrypts, code-named MAGIC, let
Washington read Tokyo's diplomatic
messages,

41
00:03:21,437 --> 00:03:22,925
often within hours.

42
00:03:23,067 --> 00:03:28,343
But MAGIC was diplomatic traffic. Japan's
main naval code was not being read in

43
00:03:28,393 --> 00:03:32,656
time, and the strike force sailed under
strict radio silence.

44
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:38,880
In September 1941, Tokyo asked its
consulate in Honolulu to report the berths
of

45
00:03:38,930 --> 00:03:43,141
warships in Pearl Harbor, using a grid of
five areas.

46
00:03:43,411 --> 00:03:48,063
Washington decoded the message in October.
It was one of many requests for

47
00:03:48,113 --> 00:03:52,457
information about American bases, and its
significance was missed.

48
00:03:53,400 --> 00:03:58,914
On November 27, Navy commanders in the
Pacific received a message that began:
"This

49
00:03:58,964 --> 00:04:02,063
dispatch is to be considered a war
warning."

50
00:04:02,333 --> 00:04:07,976
It pointed to likely Japanese moves
against the Philippines, Thailand, or
Borneo. It

51
00:04:08,026 --> 00:04:09,532
did not name Hawaii.

52
00:04:10,467 --> 00:04:17,077
On the night of December 6, Washington
decoded the first thirteen parts of a long
Japanese reply.

53
00:04:17,347 --> 00:04:22,806
The final part arrived the next morning,
with an instruction to deliver it at

54
00:04:22,856 --> 00:04:27,227
precisely one p.m. Washington time, seven
thirty in Hawaii.

55
00:04:27,497 --> 00:04:32,745
A warning from General George Marshall
went by commercial telegraph. It reached
the

56
00:04:32,795 --> 00:04:35,946
Army commander in Hawaii only after the
attack.

57
00:04:36,900 --> 00:04:39,351
That morning, two more warnings came and
went.

58
00:04:39,621 --> 00:04:44,854
At about a quarter to seven, the destroyer
U.S.S. Ward fired on a midget submarine

59
00:04:44,904 --> 00:04:50,595
near the harbor entrance and sank it. Her
report moved slowly up the chain of
command.

60
00:04:50,933 --> 00:04:57,141
At 7:02, at the Opana radar station,
Privates Joseph Lockard and George Elliott

61
00:04:57,191 --> 00:05:01,776
detected a large formation about 132 miles
to the north.

62
00:05:02,046 --> 00:05:07,592
The duty officer, Lieutenant Kermit Tyler,
believed it was a flight of B-17 bombers

63
00:05:07,642 --> 00:05:11,432
expected from California. The warning went
no further.

64
00:05:15,167 --> 00:05:20,601
The strike force, the Kido Butai, was
commanded by Vice Admiral Chuichi Nagumo.

65
00:05:20,871 --> 00:05:27,365
At its heart were six aircraft carriers:
Akagi, Kaga, Soryu, Hiryu, Shokaku, and
Zuikaku.

66
00:05:27,600 --> 00:05:32,672
They were escorted by two battleships, two
heavy cruisers, a light cruiser, and nine

67
00:05:32,722 --> 00:05:37,263
destroyers, with submarines scouting ahead
and tankers to refuel at sea.

68
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:43,144
On November 26, the force left Hitokappu
Bay in the Kuril Islands and steered east

69
00:05:43,194 --> 00:05:48,551
across the empty northern Pacific, far
from shipping lanes, in winter seas.

70
00:05:49,400 --> 00:05:55,758
On December 2, the fleet received the
coded signal confirming the attack: "Climb
Mount Niitaka."

71
00:05:56,600 --> 00:06:03,765
Before dawn on December 7, some 230 miles
north of Oahu, the carriers turned into
the wind.

72
00:06:07,300 --> 00:06:12,639
At six a.m., the first wave began to
launch: one hundred eighty-three aircraft,

73
00:06:12,689 --> 00:06:17,204
torpedo bombers, level bombers, dive
bombers, and Zero fighters.

74
00:06:17,474 --> 00:06:20,842
The air attack was led by Commander Mitsuo
Fuchida.

75
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:23,198
Over Oahu the formation divided.

76
00:06:23,468 --> 00:06:28,633
Dive bombers and fighters struck the
airfields, Wheeler, Hickam, Ford Island,
and

77
00:06:28,683 --> 00:06:35,013
Kaneohe, where American planes stood
parked wingtip to wingtip as a precaution
against sabotage.

78
00:06:35,667 --> 00:06:42,703
At 7:53, Fuchida radioed the signal that
surprise had been achieved: "Tora! Tora!
Tora!"

79
00:06:43,100 --> 00:06:48,678
Seven battleships lay moored along
Battleship Row, beside Ford Island. An
eighth,

80
00:06:48,728 --> 00:06:50,962
Pennsylvania, sat in dry dock.

81
00:06:51,232 --> 00:06:54,047
Torpedo bombers came in low across the
harbor.

82
00:06:54,100 --> 00:06:59,555
Oklahoma was hit by several torpedoes and
capsized within minutes. West Virginia

83
00:06:59,605 --> 00:07:01,550
sank upright at her moorings.

84
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:07,072
Shortly after eight o'clock, an armor-
piercing bomb struck U.S.S. Arizona and

85
00:07:07,122 --> 00:07:10,284
detonated her forward ammunition magazine.

86
00:07:10,554 --> 00:07:15,526
The explosion tore the ship apart. One
thousand one hundred seventy-seven of her

87
00:07:15,576 --> 00:07:19,807
crew were killed, nearly half of all
Americans who died that day.

88
00:07:20,500 --> 00:07:26,743
At about 8:55, a second wave of one
hundred sixty-seven aircraft arrived.
Surprise

89
00:07:26,793 --> 00:07:30,627
was gone, and they met heavy anti-aircraft
fire.

90
00:07:30,897 --> 00:07:35,267
Nevada, the only battleship to get
underway, was bombed as she moved down the

91
00:07:35,317 --> 00:07:38,854
channel, and was beached to keep the
harbor entrance clear.

92
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:44,037
By ten o'clock, the attackers were flying
back to their carriers. Nagumo chose not

93
00:07:44,087 --> 00:07:45,788
to launch another strike.

94
00:07:46,058 --> 00:07:51,170
The fuel tanks, repair shops, and
submarine base were left largely intact.
And the

95
00:07:51,220 --> 00:07:54,833
Pacific Fleet's aircraft carriers had not
been in port.

96
00:07:58,567 --> 00:08:03,166
In less than two hours, two thousand four
hundred and three Americans were killed,

97
00:08:03,216 --> 00:08:08,299
sixty-eight of them civilians, and one
thousand one hundred seventy-eight were
wounded.

98
00:08:08,569 --> 00:08:13,000
Eight battleships were damaged, four of
them sunk. More than one hundred eighty

99
00:08:13,050 --> 00:08:14,562
aircraft were destroyed.

100
00:08:15,100 --> 00:08:20,244
Aboard West Virginia, Mess Attendant
Second Class Doris "Dorie" Miller helped
carry

101
00:08:20,294 --> 00:08:24,011
his mortally wounded captain, Mervyn
Bennion, to shelter.

102
00:08:24,281 --> 00:08:29,654
Then he manned an anti-aircraft machine
gun he had not been trained to use. Miller

103
00:08:29,704 --> 00:08:34,945
became the first African American to
receive the Navy Cross. Captain Bennion
was

104
00:08:34,995 --> 00:08:36,931
awarded the Medal of Honor.

105
00:08:37,167 --> 00:08:43,030
At Kaneohe, Chief Aviation Ordnanceman
John Finn fired a machine gun from an
exposed

106
00:08:43,080 --> 00:08:49,637
position through the attack, despite
multiple wounds. He, too, received the
Medal of Honor.

107
00:08:49,900 --> 00:08:53,563
Inside the capsized Oklahoma, men were
trapped in the dark.

108
00:08:53,833 --> 00:08:59,344
Civilian navy yard workers, organized by
Julio DeCastro, cut through the hull for

109
00:08:59,394 --> 00:09:03,594
more than a day. They brought out thirty-
two sailors alive.

110
00:09:03,933 --> 00:09:09,588
Japan lost twenty-nine aircraft and five
midget submarines. Sixty-four Japanese

111
00:09:09,638 --> 00:09:15,365
servicemen were killed. One submariner,
Ensign Kazuo Sakamaki, was captured, the

112
00:09:15,415 --> 00:09:18,092
first Japanese prisoner of the war.

113
00:09:18,362 --> 00:09:23,508
Over Kaneohe, Lieutenant Fusata Iida's
fighter was hit and leaking fuel. He
turned

114
00:09:23,558 --> 00:09:28,839
back toward the air station and crashed
there. A marker stands at the site today.

115
00:09:32,600 --> 00:09:38,041
The next day, President Franklin D.
Roosevelt addressed a joint session of
Congress.

116
00:09:38,311 --> 00:09:44,098
"Yesterday, December 7th, 1941, a date
which will live in infamy, the United
States

117
00:09:44,148 --> 00:09:51,190
of America was suddenly and deliberately
attacked by naval and air forces of the
Empire of Japan."

118
00:09:52,133 --> 00:09:56,825
Within the hour, Congress declared war on
Japan: eighty-two to nothing in the

119
00:09:56,875 --> 00:10:00,411
Senate, three hundred eighty-eight to one
in the House.

120
00:10:00,681 --> 00:10:05,582
On December 11, Germany and Italy declared
war on the United States.

121
00:10:05,833 --> 00:10:11,281
The attack united a divided nation.
Recruiting stations filled. Factories that
had

122
00:10:11,331 --> 00:10:14,833
built cars turned to tanks, engines, and
aircraft.

123
00:10:15,700 --> 00:10:20,943
At Pearl Harbor, salvage crews raised and
repaired most of the damaged ships. West

124
00:10:20,993 --> 00:10:24,628
Virginia, sunk at her moorings, would
return to fight.

125
00:10:24,898 --> 00:10:30,076
And American shipyards launched dozens of
new aircraft carriers, large and small.

126
00:10:33,633 --> 00:10:39,768
Tactically, Pearl Harbor was a stunning
Japanese success. Strategically, it was a
catastrophe.

127
00:10:40,038 --> 00:10:44,803
The battleships it targeted were losing
their central place in naval warfare. The

128
00:10:44,853 --> 00:10:49,677
carriers it missed, the base it left
working, and the industrial power it
provoked

129
00:10:49,727 --> 00:10:51,125
would decide the war.

130
00:10:51,867 --> 00:10:57,340
Six months later, at Midway, American
aviators sank four of the six carriers
that

131
00:10:57,390 --> 00:11:01,427
had attacked Pearl Harbor: Akagi, Kaga,
Soryu, and Hiryu.

132
00:11:01,667 --> 00:11:07,894
A famous line has Yamamoto lamenting that
Japan had awakened a sleeping giant. There

133
00:11:07,944 --> 00:11:12,877
is no evidence he ever said it. The words
come from a 1970 film.

134
00:11:13,147 --> 00:11:15,295
But the history they describe is real.

135
00:11:15,667 --> 00:11:22,277
On September 2, 1945, Japan signed the
instrument of surrender aboard U.S.S.

136
00:11:22,327 --> 00:11:24,405
Missouri in Tokyo Bay.

137
00:11:24,675 --> 00:11:30,275
Among the American ships present was West
Virginia, raised from the mud of Pearl
Harbor.

138
00:11:30,633 --> 00:11:36,342
Today, the wreck of U.S.S. Arizona still
rests in Pearl Harbor, with more than nine

139
00:11:36,392 --> 00:11:39,039
hundred of her crew entombed inside.

140
00:11:39,309 --> 00:11:44,319
A white memorial spans the hull, honoring
the men who died on a Sunday morning that

141
00:11:44,369 --> 00:11:46,498
changed the course of history.

